{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RD1DmZaaUUshh8i14Bp1m9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://github.com/kubernetes/community/blob/master/governance.md\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes/community)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Community and Collaboration\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"sigs\", \"working-groups\", \"committees\", \"steering-committee\", \"subprojects\", \"community-principles\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Kubernetes Governance (github.com/kubernetes/community/blob/master/governance.md)\n9\t\n10\t## Principles\n11\tThe Kubernetes community adheres to the following principles: Open — Kubernetes is open source; Welcoming and respectful — see the Code of Conduct; Transparent and accessible — work and collaboration should be done in public; Merit — ideas and contributions are accepted according to their technical merit and alignment with project objectives, scope, and design principles.\n12\t\n13\t## Community groups\n14\tThe Kubernetes project is organized primarily into Special Interest Groups, or SIGs. Each SIG is comprised of members from multiple companies and organizations, with a common purpose of advancing the project with respect to a specific topic. SIGs may be vertical (Network, Storage, Node), horizontal (Scalability, Architecture), or project-support oriented (Testing, Release, Docs). SIGs must have at least one and ideally two SIG chairs at any given time. SIG chairs are intended to be organizers and facilitators, responsible for the operation of the SIG and for communication and coordination with the other SIGs and the rest of the project.\n15\t\n16\tWithin SIGs, specific work is divided into subprojects, each with designated owners who serve as technical leaders for their respective areas.\n17\t\n18\tWorking groups are primarily used to facilitate topics of discussion that are in scope for Kubernetes but that cross SIG lines. They are short-lived or address issues spanning multiple SIGs.\n19\t\n20\tCommittees do not have open membership and do not always operate in the open. They are formed by the steering committee for specific topics requiring discretion (for example Security, Code of Conduct), have charters and chairs, and report periodically to the steering committee.\n21\t\n22\tThe Steering Committee holds overall project governance; contributor roles (member, reviewer, approver) are defined in the separate community membership document.\n23\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bdbd2677-ed1a-46d2-8a51-553b92d3c7e0","uuid":"bb24379b-47d9-47a5-b74d-54e171034933","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:23:21.466Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-community-governance-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://github.com/kubernetes/community/blob/master/governance.md\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T22:30:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes/community)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D4 Community and Collaboration\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"sigs\", \"working-groups\", \"committees\", \"steering-committee\", \"subprojects\", \"community-principles\"]\n---\n# Kubernetes Governance (github.com/kubernetes/community/blob/master/governance.md)\n\n## Principles\nThe Kubernetes community adheres to the following principles: Open — Kubernetes is open source; Welcoming and respectful — see the Code of Conduct; Transparent and accessible — work and collaboration should be done in public; Merit — ideas and contributions are accepted according to their technical merit and alignment with project objectives, scope, and design principles.\n\n## Community groups\nThe Kubernetes project is organized primarily into Special Interest Groups, or SIGs. Each SIG is comprised of members from multiple companies and organizations, with a common purpose of advancing the project with respect to a specific topic. SIGs may be vertical (Network, Storage, Node), horizontal (Scalability, Architecture), or project-support oriented (Testing, Release, Docs). SIGs must have at least one and ideally two SIG chairs at any given time. SIG chairs are intended to be organizers and facilitators, responsible for the operation of the SIG and for communication and coordination with the other SIGs and the rest of the project.\n\nWithin SIGs, specific work is divided into subprojects, each with designated owners who serve as technical leaders for their respective areas.\n\nWorking groups are primarily used to facilitate topics of discussion that are in scope for Kubernetes but that cross SIG lines. They are short-lived or address issues spanning multiple SIGs.\n\nCommittees do not have open membership and do not always operate in the open. They are formed by the steering committee for specific topics requiring discretion (for example Security, Code of Conduct), have charters and chairs, and report periodically to the steering committee.\n\nThe Steering Committee holds overall project governance; contributor roles (member, reviewer, approver) are defined in the separate community membership document.\n","numLines":23,"startLine":1,"totalLines":23}}}
