{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R1AM1Ce7CKtB15KAuJ67Qm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/\"\n3\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:15:00-0400\"\n4\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\n5\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D2 Storage\", \"D2 Networking\", \"D1 Containerization\"]\n6\tconcepts_covered: [\"extension-points\", \"cri\", \"cni\", \"csi\", \"device-plugins\", \"webhooks\", \"admission-control\", \"crd\", \"api-aggregation\", \"scheduler-extensions\"]\n7\t---\n8\t# Extending Kubernetes (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/)\n9\t\n10\tKubernetes is highly configurable and extensible. As a result, there is rarely a need to fork or submit patches to the Kubernetes project code. Configuration means changing flags, local configuration files, or API resources (such as ResourceQuota, NetworkPolicy and RBAC); extensions are software components that extend and deeply integrate with Kubernetes, adapting it to support new types and new kinds of hardware.\n11\t\n12\t## Extension patterns\n13\t- Controllers — client programs that read and/or write to the Kubernetes API, following a control loop: read an object's .spec, possibly do things, and then update the object's .status.\n14\t- Webhooks — Kubernetes makes synchronous HTTP requests to a remote service (a webhook backend); this adds a potential point of failure.\n15\t- Binary plugins — Kubernetes executes external binaries; used by the kubelet (CSI storage plugins, CNI network plugins) and by kubectl (plugins).\n16\t\n17\t## Extension points\n18\t1. kubectl plugins and client credential providers.\n19\t2. API access extensions — authentication, authorization, dynamic admission control (webhooks).\n20\t3. API extensions — Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) and the API aggregation layer.\n21\t4. Scheduling extensions — scheduler plugins/profiles and custom schedulers.\n22\t5. Controllers — custom controllers managing custom or built-in resources; the operator pattern.\n23\t6. Infrastructure extensions — Device Plugins (custom hardware); Storage plugins (CSI, the Container Storage Interface, to add new storage types); Network plugins (CNI, the Container Network Interface, to implement pod networking); Container runtime (CRI, the Container Runtime Interface, to support alternative container runtimes); kubelet image credential provider plugins.\n24\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bdbd2677-ed1a-46d2-8a51-553b92d3c7e0","uuid":"ed22a20a-8345-4665-af38-1be2c0a4cb17","timestamp":"2026-08-31T13:23:26.748Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/c/dev/lodestar/Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-extending-kubernetes-2026-08-23.md","content":"---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-23T23:15:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D2 Storage\", \"D2 Networking\", \"D1 Containerization\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"extension-points\", \"cri\", \"cni\", \"csi\", \"device-plugins\", \"webhooks\", \"admission-control\", \"crd\", \"api-aggregation\", \"scheduler-extensions\"]\n---\n# Extending Kubernetes (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/)\n\nKubernetes is highly configurable and extensible. As a result, there is rarely a need to fork or submit patches to the Kubernetes project code. Configuration means changing flags, local configuration files, or API resources (such as ResourceQuota, NetworkPolicy and RBAC); extensions are software components that extend and deeply integrate with Kubernetes, adapting it to support new types and new kinds of hardware.\n\n## Extension patterns\n- Controllers — client programs that read and/or write to the Kubernetes API, following a control loop: read an object's .spec, possibly do things, and then update the object's .status.\n- Webhooks — Kubernetes makes synchronous HTTP requests to a remote service (a webhook backend); this adds a potential point of failure.\n- Binary plugins — Kubernetes executes external binaries; used by the kubelet (CSI storage plugins, CNI network plugins) and by kubectl (plugins).\n\n## Extension points\n1. kubectl plugins and client credential providers.\n2. API access extensions — authentication, authorization, dynamic admission control (webhooks).\n3. API extensions — Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) and the API aggregation layer.\n4. Scheduling extensions — scheduler plugins/profiles and custom schedulers.\n5. Controllers — custom controllers managing custom or built-in resources; the operator pattern.\n6. Infrastructure extensions — Device Plugins (custom hardware); Storage plugins (CSI, the Container Storage Interface, to add new storage types); Network plugins (CNI, the Container Network Interface, to implement pod networking); Container runtime (CRI, the Container Runtime Interface, to support alternative container runtimes); kubelet image credential provider plugins.\n","numLines":24,"startLine":1,"totalLines":24}}}
